  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 223 AM EST Fri Nov 20 2026\n\n- Potential for Fog this Morning\n\nThe potential for fog remains this morning. REFS, RRFS, HRRR, and\nRAP all focus on an axis of fog developing in Muskegon, Ottawa,\nKent, Ionia, and Clinton Counties. Fog is expected to develop\nbetween 5-7am and linger as late as 11am. Fog could be dense (1/4\nmile or less) and cause hazardous travel conditions. Areas to the\nnorth and south could see visibility reductions due to fog, but \nmay be limited to 1 to 3 miles. If traveling this morning make \nsure to give yourself plenty of time and use your low-beam \nheadlights.\n\n- Cold Front Brings Rain for Sunday, Lake Effect Possible Monday\n\nWarm temperatures and dry conditions persist until a cold front\narrives Sunday. As previously mentioned there will be plenty of\nlift with the support from upper level jet divergence, positive\nvorticity advection, and the frontal boundary. Probabilities of a\nquarter of an inch or more are focused along and west of US-131\n(40 to 60 percent) with lower probabilities to the east.\n\nBehind the front cold air will quickly advect into the region.\nDepending on if moisture is deep enough, there is the potential\nfor lake effect showers with lake surface and 850 mb temperature\ndifferences around 20 C. Currently the better potential will be\nMonday night where we could see decent lapse rates and\ntemperatures cold enough to support snow.\n\nHighs next week will largely be in the 30s to low 40s with lows \nin the 20s into Thanksgiving.\n\n- Uncertainty Remains for Midweek Precipitation Chances\n\nSpeaking of the Thanksgiving holiday, an upper level low is\nexpected to track through the Great Lakes/Ohio Valley area. At\nthis time confidence in the track and timing is very low. This \nwill impact our precipitation chances with some ensemble members \nkeeping us dry through the holiday while others bring chances for \nrain and snow. We'll continue to monitor trends during this\ntimeframe.",
